window.PH_BOOK_LOAD({"id":"17-29","translated":true,"translator":"AI (Claude)","sections":[{"slug":"17-29-01","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/17-29-01/","title_he":"א – החומרה שבאיסור יין גויים","title_en":"The Stringency of the Prohibition of Gentile Wine","body_en":"<p>Since wine is the most important and most gladdening drink, and its power to release a person from inhibitions, to draw hearts close and to arouse joy is great, the Torah commanded that it be poured as a libation together with the sacrifices, so that we may draw close to God with this special power as well. Likewise the Sages instituted that a blessing be recited over a cup of wine at every joyous occasion of a mitzva: at Kiddush and Havdala of Shabbat and festivals, at betrothal and marriage, at a brit mila and a pidyon ha-ben, so that the joy of the holiness of all these mitzvot would be revealed in the body and its sensations as well. On the other hand, since the influence of wine is great, the danger in its improper use is very great, and people of lust became addicted to drinking it and committed all the abominations while drunk. And idolaters poured it as a libation to their idols in the hope that they would help them fulfill all their desires. Therefore, the Sages were exceedingly stringent regarding wine, and when they decreed a prohibition on the wine, bread and cooked dishes of gentiles, they were stringent to regard wine that gentiles prepared, which is called \"stam yeinam\" (their ordinary wine), like wine that they poured as a libation to their idols, which is forbidden even for benefit. Not only that, but they decreed that even a Jew's wine that an idolatrous gentile touched in the manner in which they used to touch wine when pouring a libation to their idols would be forbidden for benefit.</p><p>It turns out, then, that there are two stringencies in the prohibition of wine compared with the prohibition of the bread and cooking of gentiles. The first: since wine may create excessive closeness, in order to distance Israel from assimilation they forbade even a Jew's wine that a gentile touched, even though he did not intend by his touch to pour it as a libation to idolatry. The second: wine that an idolatrous gentile touched was forbidden even for benefit and not only for drinking, like the law of wine poured as a libation to idolatry, which is forbidden for benefit by Torah law, for this is the way of the Sages, to make the prohibitions uniform, so that Israel would be able to remember the halakha and keep it without confusing the laws.</p><p>Wine that is forbidden for benefit may not be sold to a gentile or even given to him as a gift, for there is benefit even from giving a gift.<sup>1</sup></p>","notes_en":[{"n":1,"en":"The Torah warned not to eat or benefit from takrovet avoda zara (offerings to idolatry), that is, something that was offered to idolatry, as it is stated (Shemot 34:15-16): \"Lest you make a covenant with the inhabitants of the land, and they go astray after their gods and sacrifice to their gods, and one call you and you eat of his sacrifice; and you take of his daughters for your sons, and his daughters go astray after their gods and make your sons go astray after their gods\" (Ramban, Hasagot to Sefer Ha-mitzvot, negative commandment 195, and on the Torah there). Yayin nesekh, that is, wine that was poured as a libation for idolatry, is forbidden for benefit by Torah law like a sacrifice that was offered to idolatry, as it is stated (Devarim 32:38): \"Who ate the fat of their sacrifices, and drank the wine of their libations\" (Rambam, Hilkhot Ma'akhalot Asurot 11:1-2; Hilkhot Avoda Zara 7:2 [?]). And one who drinks even the smallest amount of it is punished with lashes, as it is stated (Devarim 13:18): \"And nothing of the devoted thing shall cleave to your hand.\" Before the idols, the libation of wine was performed by pouring it out before the statue or the object worshipped, and not in the presence of the statue – by agitating (shikhshukh) the wine (Rosh, Avoda Zara 4:13; and see note 9).<br>The Sages decreed regarding the \"stam yeinam\" of idolatrous gentiles, and regarding a Jew's wine that an idolatrous gentile touched, that even though it is not known that they were poured as libations to idolatry, their law would be like that of actual \"yayin nesekh,\" lest they were poured as libations to idolatry (Avoda Zara 29b, in the Mishna and Gemara). And although this concern is very remote, in order to make a safeguard against assimilation (above 27:2, n. 6), they established on the basis of this concern a prohibition of benefit for their ordinary wine and even for a Jew's wine that an idolatrous gentile touched. Moreover, they made all the laws of stam yeinam the same as the law of yayin nesekh, as is the way of the Sages, who institute enactments similar to Torah prohibitions (Ramban). Thereby it will be easier for Israel to keep the halakha, and they will not confuse the prohibitions (based on the Ran). And according to the Rashba (Torat Ha-bayit Ha-arukh 5:1), at first they decreed only a prohibition of drinking on the ordinary wine of gentiles, and afterwards, when the gentiles who poured wine as libations to their idols increased, they added a decree prohibiting benefit from all gentile wine, lest it had been poured as a libation to idolatry. However, he too agrees that the basis of the stringency regarding their ordinary wine rests on the concern for assimilation, for even when the gentile does not engage in idolatry, his touch forbids the wine for drinking, similar to the touch of idolaters in pouring their wine as a libation, as explained in the next halakha."}]},{"slug":"17-29-02","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/17-29-02/","title_he":"ב – גוי שאינו עובד עבודה זרה","title_en":"A Gentile Who Does Not Engage in Idolatry","body_en":"<p>When the gentile does not engage in idolatry, there is no concern that he poured the wine as a libation to idolatry, but the concern of assimilation exists, and therefore the Sages forbade his wine for drinking but not for benefit. That is, on the one hand, because of the special stringency regarding wine, which has the power to blur the boundaries needed for preserving Jewish distinctiveness, they were stringent to forbid wine through his touch. And on the other hand, since he does not engage in idolatry, they forbade his wine, and the wine he touched, for drinking but not for benefit. Thus the first stringency of the prohibition of wine applies to him, and therefore his touch too forbids the wine, whereas the second stringency, according to which the wine is forbidden for benefit as well, does not apply to him (SA 124:1, 6).</p><p>This is the rule: the law of a gentile who does not engage in idolatry is one degree more lenient than that of a gentile who engages in idolatry. Therefore, whatever is forbidden for benefit with an idolater is forbidden for drinking with a gentile who does not engage in idolatry, and whatever is forbidden only for drinking with an idolater is permitted with a gentile who does not engage in idolatry (SA 124:7).<sup>2</sup></p><p>As we shall learn in the next halakha, today the law of a gentile who does not engage in idolatry is more common; however, in the course of our study we shall deal mainly with the law of idolaters, with whom the Sages dealt in the Mishna and Talmud, and from this the law of a gentile who does not engage in idolatry, whose law is one degree more lenient, will be clarified.</p>","notes_en":[{"n":2,"en":"According to the Geonim, Rambam (Hilkhot Ma'akhalot Asurot 11:7) and Rashba (Torat Ha-bayit Ha-arukh 5:1), just as a ger toshav (resident alien) forbids his wine and the wine he touched for drinking but not for benefit (Avoda Zara 64b), and just as a gentile child from a family of idolaters, who does not understand the nature of idolatry, forbids the wine for drinking but not for benefit (Avoda Zara 57a), so too is the law of a gentile who does not engage in idolatry, such as a Muslim, that his wine and the wine he touched are forbidden for drinking but not for benefit. And so it was ruled in the Tur and SA 124:6. And so wrote the Radbaz (8:19), Erekh Leḥem (123:1), the Ḥida and the Ben Ish Ḥai (Year Two, Balak 1). However, some are stringent regarding wine that a gentile who does not engage in idolatry prepared, that it is forbidden for benefit, and only his touch of a Jew's wine forbids it for drinking but not for benefit (Ramban, Ra'ah and Ritva). On the other hand, according to Tosafot Rid (Avoda Zara 57a, s.v. \"Tinok\") and his grandson the Riaz, the touch of a gentile who does not engage in idolatry does not forbid a Jew's wine, and it is permitted even for drinking, and only wine that he prepared himself is forbidden for drinking.<br><b>Ger toshav:</b> According to the Rashba (Torat Ha-bayit Ha-arukh 5:1) and SA 123:2, his touch forbids the wine for drinking, like a gentile who does not engage in idolatry. And according to Tosafot (Avoda Zara 64b), Rosh (5:5) and Rema 124:2, it is permitted for drinking. And it implies that this is the law in our times as well, even though a ger toshav is not accepted by a beit din, for whoever has in practice taken upon himself to keep the seven commandments (so wrote Apei Ravrevei 79:3, and so it is implied by Rabbeinu Yeruḥam, Baḥ 124:2, and Shakh 124:4). But wine that he prepared himself is, according to all opinions, forbidden for drinking and permitted for benefit, like the law of the bread and cooked dishes of gentiles, because of the concern of intermarriage – assimilation (Rema there; Rabbi Akiva Eiger on Taz 123:3). Thus according to the Rosh and those who agree with him, there are three levels of prohibition: a) an idolatrous gentile forbids his wine and a Jew's wine that he touched for drinking and benefit. b) A gentile who does not engage in idolatry forbids his wine and a Jew's wine that he touched for drinking only. c) A ger toshav – the wine he prepared is forbidden for drinking, and the wine he touched is permitted for drinking.<br><b>A child</b> from a family of idolaters is considered like a gentile who does not engage in idolatry, whose touch forbids the wine for drinking but not for benefit. And as long as he is not accustomed to mention idolatry with intention, his law is still that of a minor (Avoda Zara 57a; SA 124:1; 123:1; Taz 1). And if he is a child from a family that does not engage in idolatry, his touch does not forbid the wine for drinking (Ben Ish Ḥai, Year Two, Balak 2). A mentally incompetent gentile from a family of idolaters forbids the wine for drinking but not for benefit (SA 124:15)."}]},{"slug":"17-29-03","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/17-29-03/","title_he":"ג – מוסלמים, נוצרים והינדואיסטים","title_en":"Muslims, Christians and Hindus","body_en":"<p>A Muslim, even though he denies the Torah, since he believes in one God without idolatrous partnership (shittuf), is not considered an idolater, and therefore the wine he prepared or the wine he touched is forbidden for drinking and permitted for benefit (as explained in note 2). And likewise is the law of a secular gentile, and likewise the law of members of the Druze religion.</p><p>Regarding Christians, the poskim disagreed. According to the stringent opinion, since they believe that \"that man\" too is God, they associate idolatry in their faith, and therefore wine they touched becomes forbidden for benefit (Rambam, Hilkhot Ma'akhalot Asurot 11:7). And some are lenient and hold that since in practice they are not accustomed to pour wine as a libation to idolatry, wine that they touch is forbidden only for drinking and not for benefit (Rashi, Rashbam). And some of the Rishonim are lenient in this because in their opinion the Christians of their time were already not considered complete idolaters, since their religion is more abstract than that of ordinary idolaters (Meiri). In practice, ab initio the custom follows the stringent opinion, and in a time of pressing need one relies on the lenient opinion. And it seems that even according to the stringent opinion, Christians who believe that \"that man\" was a messenger and not God are not considered idolaters, and according to all opinions the wine they touch is permitted for benefit.</p><p>Hindus and members of other religions whose worshippers bow down to statues and offer them offerings are considered idolaters. However, according to the approach of the lenient opinion, since they are not accustomed to pour wine as a libation to their idols, their touch of wine forbids it for drinking but not for benefit. In a time of pressing need, one may rely on their opinion.<sup>3</sup></p>","notes_en":[{"n":3,"en":"The Rambam wrote (Hilkhot Avoda Zara 9:4, and in the Commentary on the Mishna, Avoda Zara 1:3) that the Christians are idolaters, and their touch forbids the wine for benefit, and many agreed with his opinion. And the Meiri (Avoda Zara 2a; 57a) holds that they are not considered idolaters, and therefore their touch forbids for drinking but not for benefit. And there are additional poskim who were lenient to trade with Christians on their festival day because they are not complete idolaters (see SA YD 148:12; Rema OḤ 156:1, and the commentators there; and Shakh 151:7).<br>Returning to the law of wine: according to many, since the Christians are idolaters, wine that they touched is forbidden for benefit, and this is the opinion of the Rambam (Hilkhot Ma'akhalot Asurot 11:7), Ramban, Ritva, Rashba (Torat Ha-bayit 5:1), SA 123:1; Knesset Ha-gedola, Hagahot Beit Yosef 8; Ḥesed La-alafim 1; Rabbi Ḥayim Palagi in Ru'aḥ Ḥayim 1; Ben Ish Ḥai, Year Two, Balak 4. On the other hand, many were lenient to permit it for benefit in order to prevent loss: according to Rashi, Rashbam and Rivan, the touch of Christians does not forbid the wine for benefit, since they are not accustomed to pour libations, and in a time of need they even permitted collecting debts in the form of wine that gentiles made. And so wrote the Semag and Agur, and Tosafot Rid wrote similarly. And according to Tosafot (Avoda Zara 57b, s.v. \"Le-afukei mi-de-Rav\") and Rosh (Avoda Zara 4:7), in a time of pressing need one may rely on the lenient opinion in this. Similarly, the Rema ruled (123:1) that in a case of loss, their wine and their touch are forbidden for drinking and permitted for benefit. And this is agreed upon by the Ashkenazi Aḥaronim. The poskim in Islamic lands too relied on the lenient opinion in a case of enormous loss, as wrote Yafeh La-lev vol. 3, YD 132:1, and Ben Ish Ḥai, Year Two, Balak 4. The status of Buddhists is between Muslims and Christians: on the one hand they have statues; on the other hand, they do not worship them, and in any case it is clear that they are not accustomed to pour libations to idols."}]},{"slug":"17-29-04","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/17-29-04/","title_he":"ד – מהו יין","title_en":"What Is Wine","body_en":"<p>As long as the liquid coming out of the grapes is still mixed with the seeds and skins, it does not have the law of wine, and if a gentile touched it – it does not become forbidden. Once the liquid has begun to separate from them, that is, it has flowed to the side where it remains without seeds and skins, it has the law of wine. And then all the other liquids in the winepress also have the law of wine, and if a gentile touched the liquids, or the wet seeds or skins in the winepress, he has forbidden everything in the winepress. Not only that, but even if one cup of wine, strained of seeds and skins, was taken from the winepress, from that moment the law of wine applies to everything that remains in the winepress (SA 123:17-18).</p><p>Wine that has turned into vinegar does not become forbidden through a gentile's touch. When there is a doubt whether the wine has already become complete vinegar, it is tested by pouring it on the ground: if it bubbles and ferments due to its contact with the limestone material – this is a sign that it has become vinegar (SA 123:6).</p><p>The wine that becomes forbidden through libation or a gentile's touch is wine in its natural form, but if it has changed from its nature, it does not become forbidden, for the gentiles were not accustomed to pour it as a libation. Therefore, wine diluted with water, as long as the wine is more than a sixth of the water, has the law of wine, and a gentile's touch forbids it, since it is the nature of wine that it is customarily diluted. But if its proportion is lower than this, the law of wine no longer applies to it (SA 134:5).</p><p>Similarly, wine into which sugar or honey was mixed to sweeten it, or pepper to make it sharp, if its taste has changed from its natural taste, does not become forbidden through a gentile's touch, since the gentiles did not pour it as a libation to their idols. But wine into which a little sugar or honey or another flavor was mixed, and its taste changed only slightly, if a gentile touches it – it becomes forbidden (SA 123:4).</p><p>In general, the wines whose natural taste is preserved are dry wines, semi-dry wines, and wines that are slightly sweet. And although a little sugar is mixed into them or a little alcohol is added to them, their natural taste is preserved. But in very sweet wines, such as the simple sweet wines, much sugar is mixed in and their taste has changed, and therefore a gentile's touch does not forbid them.<sup>4</sup></p>","notes_en":[{"n":4,"en":"The basis of the law that wine whose taste was changed does not become forbidden is in the Yerushalmi, Avoda Zara 2:3. And the Rambam wrote (Hilkhot Ma'akhalot Asurot 11:10) that even if a little honey or leaven was mixed into the wine, it does not become forbidden, because it is not fit for libation on the altar. However, according to Ramban, Rashba, Rosh, Ran and Ritva, even if a little honey or leaven was mixed into the wine, as long as its taste did not change, it becomes forbidden through a gentile's touch, because the gentiles were accustomed to pour libations even of wine unfit for the altar, just as they used to pour libations of diluted wine. And so it was ruled in the SA and Rema (123:4), that only if its taste changed does it not have the law of yayin nesekh. Thus far. And some are stringent and hold that only if the taste of wine has been lost from it does it not become forbidden (Ḥavot Ya'ir and Ḥatam Sofer). And most poskim wrote according to the SA and Rema. However, when a slight sweetness is felt in the wine, it is impossible to distinguish whether it is from the nature of the wine or due to added sugar that changed its taste, and thus this is wine whose taste is natural.<br>If a Jew mixed wine into a cooked dish or a cake, if a gentile touches the dish or the cake, they do not become forbidden (SA 123:5). Wine that congealed does not become forbidden through a gentile's touch, and if it became liquid again, its law reverts to being that of wine (Rema 123:5; Shakh 12)."}]},{"slug":"17-29-05","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/17-29-05/","title_he":"ה – יין מבושל","title_en":"Cooked Wine","body_en":"<p>Wine that has been cooked does not become forbidden through a gentile's touch, since idolaters did not pour cooked wine as a libation to their idols, and wine that is not fit for libation does not become forbidden through a gentile's touch. However, if the gentile touched the wine before it was cooked, since it had already become forbidden, the cooking will not save it from its prohibition (Avoda Zara 29b).</p><p>The poskim disagreed as to when wine is considered cooked, and many definitions have been given for this. In practice there are two opinions. According to the stringent opinion, from the moment its taste changes noticeably, as happens after the wine boils and remains on the fire until its liquids are noticeably reduced due to evaporation. And according to the lenient opinion, from the moment it reaches a heat that begins to change its taste, and this happens after the wine reaches a heat of about seventy degrees, when the alcohol in the wine begins to evaporate. And since the prohibition of gentile wine is rabbinic, the halakha follows the lenient opinion.</p><p>However, the poskim of our time disagreed regarding pasteurized wine and grape juice, that is, where the grape juice or wine was heated to a temperature of about seventy to eighty degrees in order to disinfect it of bacteria and yeasts, so that it can no longer ferment and will remain in its taste without change. Some are stringent because the taste does not change through this. And although the alcohol begins to evaporate, since the pasteurization is performed in closed vessels, the alcohol returns to the wine, and consequently its taste does not change. Moreover, one should take into account the stringent opinion, which holds that only if much of the liquid of the wine has evaporated and its taste has changed noticeably is it not subject to the prohibition (Rav Shlomo Zalman Auerbach, Or Le-Tziyon, Rav Yosef Shalom Elyashiv). On the other hand, some hold that since in practice the wine reached a level of heat that cooks it, and the alcohol in it began to evaporate, it has changed from its nature and is not subject to the prohibition. And although afterwards the alcohol that evaporated returned to the wine, since there was a stage at which it left the wine, its law is that of cooked wine. In addition, connoisseurs feel that its taste is slightly impaired (Igrot Moshe, Rav Goren, Yabi'a Omer, Minḥat Yitzḥak). And since the basis of the prohibition is rabbinic, the halakha follows the lenient opinion. Those who wish to be meticulous are stringent. However, in a situation where an insult might be caused to someone whom it is not proper to insult, even one who wishes to be meticulous should act according to the lenient opinion.<sup>5</sup></p>","notes_en":[{"n":5,"en":"In Avoda Zara 29b it is explained that cooked wine is not subject to the prohibition of nesekh. Some say this is because cooked wine is unfit for libation upon the altar, since it has changed from its nature, and so too the gentiles were accustomed not to pour it as a libation (Rambam, Hilkhot Ma'akhalot Asurot 11:9-10; Ran and Tur). And some say it is because it has changed in its taste and its name from ordinary wine to cooked wine, and therefore the gentiles were not accustomed to pour it as a libation to idolatry (Ramban and Rashba). And some say it is because cooked wine was not common then, and therefore they did not include it in the decree (Rosh and Ritva).<br>The main approach in defining cooking is that the wine was heated on the fire until it \"boiled\" (ratḥ). So wrote many Rishonim in the name of the Geonim, among them: Rabbi Yitzḥak ibn Gi'at, Teruma, Or Zarua, Shibbolei Ha-leket and Orḥot Ḥayim. And so it was ruled in the Tur and SA 123:3. In explaining \"ratḥ,\" some say that it means cooking, as in the laws of cooking on Shabbat, that is, that it reached a heat at which the hand recoils (yad soledet bo), about 70 degrees, when the alcohol in it also begins to evaporate and consequently its taste also begins to change (Igrot Moshe, Rav Goren, Yabi'a Omer, Minḥat Yitzḥak). And some say that it means that it produces bubbles of boiling, and then this approach is like the approach of the Ramban, who wrote, based on the Yerushalmi, that the cooking must reduce the wine from its measure (Ran, Tashbetz, Shakh 123:7).<br>And some say that cooked wine is wine that has changed in its taste or its smell (Meiri). And it is possible that his approach too corresponds to the approach of the Ramban, and perhaps he is more stringent, that it is not enough that it reach boiling, but it must remain boiling for some time. And some are even more stringent, that cooked wine is wine that has been reduced until it has become as thick as honey (an opinion in the Meiri; Ḥida in the name of Maharam Lonzano; Ben Ish Ḥai).<br>A great question arose regarding pasteurized wine and grape juice, that is, heated to a temperature of slightly more than 70 degrees in order to disinfect them of bacteria and yeasts. Some say that the halakha follows those who hold that cooked wine is wine whose taste or smell changed, or at least that produced bubbles of boiling and whose measure was reduced. But in pasteurization it does not reach actual boiling, nor does it reduce the wine, since it is done in closed vessels, so that whatever evaporates returns to the wine, and therefore pasteurized wine does not have the law of cooked wine (Rav Shlomo Zalman Auerbach in Minḥat Shlomo 1:25; Rav Yosef Shalom Elyashiv in Kovetz Teshuvot 1:75; Or Le-Tziyon vol. 2, 20:18). And some say that the main approach in halakha, based on SA 123:3, is that whatever reached a heat at which the hand recoils is considered boiled and cooked. And its taste also changes slightly, since the alcohol begins to evaporate at this heat. And although it returns to the wine, since for a time it evaporated, the law of wine has already departed from it. Moreover, connoisseurs sense a difference between pasteurized and unpasteurized wine (Igrot Moshe YD 2:52; 3:31; Rav Goren, Terumat Ha-goren 2:17-18; Yabi'a Omer YD 8:15; Minḥat Yitzḥak 7:61).<br>The stringent ones further relied on the words of the Rosh (Avoda Zara 2:13), who asked: since the prohibition of gentile wine is because of their daughters, cooked wine too should be forbidden. And he answered that perhaps because it is not common, the Sages did not decree concerning it. The stringent ones learned from this that today pasteurized wine becomes forbidden through a gentile's touch, since it is common. However, on the other hand, many hold that even according to the Rosh, cooked wine today is permitted, since in practice the decree did not apply to it, for in the time of Ḥazal it was not common, and we may not add decrees on our own (Avnei Nezer YD 116:4; Yabi'a Omer there). In addition, they raised a difficulty with the Rosh's question: only wine that gentiles made is forbidden because of their daughters, but wine that a gentile touched, since it is forbidden because of yayin nesekh, does not become forbidden if it is cooked (Rabbi Akiva Eiger 123:3). Indeed, some say that the Rosh was speaking of cooked wine that a gentile prepared (Prisha 123:8). And some explained that the Rosh was dealing with the prohibition of drinking with a gentile at his party, which does not apply to strong drinks of rare kinds (Responsa Mabit 1:208, s.v. \"Ra'iti talmid ḥakham\")."}]},{"slug":"17-29-06","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/17-29-06/","title_he":"ו – נגיעה ביין","title_en":"Touching Wine","body_en":"<p>As we learned (halakha 1), the Sages decreed that any touch by an idolatrous gentile of a Jew's wine, similar to the manner in which the gentiles were accustomed to pour libations to idols, forbids the wine for benefit. Their manner of libation was to agitate the wine, that is, to move it with a hand or a stick. Likewise they were accustomed to pour libations by touching with the mouth while drinking, for while drinking they intended to move the wine slightly and thereby pour it as a libation to the idols. And even when it is clear that the gentile did not intend by his touching or his drinking to pour the wine as a libation to idols, since he is an idolater and touched the wine in the way idolaters used to pour libations to idols, the wine is forbidden for benefit. And if the touching was done by a gentile who does not engage in idolatry, the wine is forbidden for drinking but not for benefit (SA 124:11).<sup>6</sup></p><p>If he touched the wine without intention, for example his hand was inadvertently dipped into the wine, or he thought it was beer and intended to touch the beer – the wine is forbidden for drinking but not for benefit (SA 124:10). If the unintentional touching was by a gentile who does not engage in idolatry – the wine is permitted for drinking (SA 124:7).</p><p>When there is a doubt whether the gentile touched the wine intentionally, one is lenient, since it is a rabbinic doubt (Shakh 124:33). And likewise in any doubt concerning the law of yayin nesekh, one is lenient.<sup>7</sup></p>","notes_en":[{"n":6,"en":"According to all opinions the libation is the agitating of the wine, but according to SA 124:11, based on Rambam (Hilkhot Ma'akhalot Asurot 12:1) and other Rishonim, only agitating that moves the wine significantly forbids it, but a touch that moves it slightly forbids the wine for drinking only. And according to Ramban, Rashba and Ritva, even touching without agitating forbids it for benefit, for every touch moves the wine in some way. And so wrote the Taz 124:11; Knesset Ha-gedola; Gra 31; Ḥokhmat Adam 76:7. According to SA 124:11, even touching with a bare foot forbids it for benefit, since they also poured libations in this way. And according to the Rema, touching with the foot is not respectful and therefore is not fit for libation, and consequently its law is like the law of touching with other limbs, which forbids for drinking but not for benefit. And if a gentile who does not engage in idolatry touched the wine with his other limbs, it is permitted for drinking. <b>If he drank from the wine with a straw</b>, all the wine remaining in the vessel becomes forbidden (SA 124:11). And some permit it if the wine contains sixty times the wine that was in the straw and returned to the vessel, for in their view the prohibition is not because of moving the wine but because of the wine that touched his mouth (Taz 124:32)."},{"n":7,"en":"Since the prohibition of their ordinary wine is rabbinic, the rule is that in every doubt one is lenient. And it was said in the Yerushalmi (Sanhedrin 4:7): \"One is not exacting with yayin nesekh,\" implying that one is lenient even when the doubt leans toward stringency, as the Rosh wrote (Responsa, Kelal 19:1): \"We are very lenient with yayin nesekh, and on the basis of slight reasoning they attribute toward leniency in several places in the Gemara, and likewise in the responsa of the great authorities.\" Admittedly, there were pious individuals who took care not to drink even wine that a gentile had looked at, as mentioned in the Shelah (Kedushat Ha-akhila 15). However, according to halakha there is no prohibition at all in this. Moreover, even when there is a doubt whether the gentile touched the wine – one is lenient (Ra'avya, Ra'avad, Rashba, Meiri, Rema 128:2, and many others). And likewise in a doubt arising from a dispute among the poskim – the halakha follows the lenient one (Rashi, Ritva, Ramban, Tur, SA 129:11, and many others).<br>As we learned, intentional touching with the hand or with a stick forbids the wine for benefit. However, without intention there is a difference: touching with the hand forbids for drinking, and with a stick does not forbid at all (SA 124:24). And if he touched with a stick for a certain purpose, such as to measure the wine, the wine is forbidden for drinking but not for benefit, because although he intended to touch the wine, the touching had another intention that is not libation (Rif, Rambam, SA 124:19). And some say that this is also the law for touching with the hand for a certain purpose, that it forbids for drinking but not for benefit (Ra'avad, Rashba, Rosh, Ran, and so ruled the Rema in a case of loss). And if a gentile who does not engage in idolatry touched the wine by means of a utensil for a certain purpose, the wine is permitted for drinking (Maharalbaḥ; Darkhei Moshe 124:17)."}]},{"slug":"17-29-07","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/17-29-07/","title_he":"ז – שכשוך יין על ידי נענוע הכלי","title_en":"Agitating Wine by Shaking the Vessel","body_en":"<p>Some say that idolaters had an additional manner of libation, by agitating wine in an open vessel, that is, shaking the vessel forcefully in order to move the wine inside it. And the Sages decreed that if an idolatrous gentile agitated it in this manner, even not for the sake of libation, the wine is forbidden for benefit. And if he does not engage in idolatry, the wine is forbidden for drinking. But if the vessel containing the wine was closed, even with a temporary stopper or a plastic bag, the wine does not become forbidden at all, for there is no manner of libation with a closed vessel (Rambam, Rivam, Ramban, Rosh, SA and Rema 124:17). And if an idolatrous gentile lifted the open vessel and walked with it, and even went up stairs, and the wine in the vessel moved forcefully, since it moved due to walking – the wine is permitted. Likewise, if the wine moved as a result of pulling out the bottle's cork – the wine is permitted. Thus, in their opinion, only shaking the vessel and agitating the wine inside it without reason forbids the wine.</p><p>On the other hand, some are lenient and hold that it was not customary to pour libations with a vessel, and therefore moving wine inside a vessel does not forbid it (Ra'avad, Rashba, Rabbeinu Yeruḥam, Meiri, Rivash, Maharashdam, Baḥ, Taz and Knesset Ha-gedola).</p><p>In practice, regarding an idolatrous gentile, ab initio the custom follows the stringent opinion, and in a case of loss one is lenient regarding benefit. And with a gentile who does not engage in idolatry, one is not stringent about agitating in a vessel at all.<sup>8</sup></p>","notes_en":[{"n":8,"en":"Avoda Zara 60a: a gentile who carried an open vessel containing wine, and there is a reasonable concern that while carrying it he touched the wine, for example the vessel was full to the brim, the wine is forbidden for benefit. And if there is no concern that he touched the wine, the wine is permitted (SA 125:10; see Halakha Berura, Avoda Zara 60a, notes 4-5). According to the lenient ones, because there is no prohibition of agitating in a vessel, and according to the stringent ones, because only moving without reason forbids, and here the moving was due to walking. And even if the moving was forceful, it is permitted (Drisha 125:5; Rashal; Rashdam; Taz 125:13, according to the Rosh; and others. And some understood from several poskim that they were stringent when it moved forcefully).<br>Regarding a gentile who does not engage in idolatry, one should not be stringent at all about agitating in a vessel. Admittedly, some forbid it for drinking (Tevu'ot Shemesh YD 56). However, the opinion of most poskim is that agitating in a vessel is like touching by means of another object without intention, which does not forbid at all. And some were lenient in this in a time of need regarding Christians, since they are not accustomed to pour libations (Noda Bi-Yehuda, Tinyana, YD 69-70; Rabbi Akiva Eiger on Rema 124:17; Pitḥei Teshuva 3).<br>If he agitated the wine in a bottle while it was resting on the table, some forbid it (SA 124:17), and according to many it is permitted, for there is no libation without lifting (Rema there). And together with the opinion of those who hold that there is no libation with a vessel, it should be permitted. The Responsa Maharshal 76 wrote that even if the vessel containing the wine was wet with wine on its outside, touching the vessel from the outside does not forbid the wine, since moisture that is \"tofeaḥ al menat lehatpiaḥ\" (wet enough to moisten something else) does not create a connection from the outside to the inside, and it is also not clear that the moisture is continuous."}]},{"slug":"17-29-08","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/17-29-08/","title_he":"ח – כוחו – מזיגת יין","title_en":"Koḥo (His Force) – Pouring Wine","body_en":"<p>If an idolatrous gentile poured wine from a bottle into a cup, all the wine in the cup and in the bottle is forbidden for drinking and permitted for benefit. It is not forbidden for benefit, since he did not touch it with his hand, nor did he agitate it in the vessel. And it is forbidden for drinking because the Sages forbade wine that the gentile poured by his force (koḥo). And since the stream came out of the bottle continuously into the cup, a \"nitzok\" (poured stream) connection was created between the wine in the cup and the wine that remained in the bottle, and the wine in the bottle too is forbidden for drinking. However, in a situation of great loss, for example where he poured the wine from a large vessel, the wine that remained in the large vessel is kosher for drinking (SA and Rema 125:1; 126:1-2).<sup>9</sup></p><p>And if a gentile who does not engage in idolatry poured from the bottle into the cup, according to many, the wine in the cup and in the bottle is permitted for drinking (Shakh 124:11; Dagul Me-revava, Rabbi Akiva Eiger, Erekh Ha-shulḥan, Ish Matzli'aḥ, Ḥazon Ish). And some are stringent and forbid the wine in the cup for drinking and permit the wine that remained in the bottle (Taz; Yabi'a Omer YD 1:11). And although according to the rules of halakha one should rule according to the lenient opinion, many are accustomed to be stringent. And some are even accustomed to be stringent regarding what remained in the bottle.</p><p>In practice, the main ruling follows the lenient opinion, but one should not employ gentiles to pour wine, since it is difficult to supervise that they do not touch the wine. Therefore the custom is that when the waiter is a gentile, even though he does not engage in idolatry, the diners open the bottle and pour for themselves. However, after the fact, if the waiter opened the bottle and poured for them, the wine is kosher, and all the more so the wine that remained in the bottle is kosher. For as long as they did not see him agitating the wine in the bottle or touching the wine in it, it is permitted to drink the wine (as explained above in halakha 6 and note 7; and the details of the halakha here are below, at the end of the note).<sup>10</sup></p>","notes_en":[{"n":9,"en":"In Avoda Zara 58a-b it was said that wine that a gentile poured, that is, that was poured by his force, even though it is not known to us that he touched it – is forbidden for benefit. Some say this is because this is the manner of libation, and it is included in the decree of stam yeinam (Rambam, Hilkhot Ma'akhalot Asurot 12:2, according to the Kesef Mishneh; Ri, Ra'avan, Ra'avad, Rashba, Ritva). And some say that wine that a gentile poured is permitted for benefit, for only next to the statue do they pour libations in this way, and they forbade it for drinking in order to keep away from their daughters. And what was said in the Gemara, that wine a gentile poured is forbidden for benefit, refers to a situation in which there is a concern that he touched it (Rosh in the name of Rashi and Rabbeinu Tam; Ramban and Ra'ah). And so it was ruled in SA 125:1.<br>In order to understand the law of the wine that remained in the bottle from which the gentile poured, one must first state that according to Rav Huna (Avoda Zara 72a), nitzok is considered a connection with regard to yayin nesekh (even though it is not a connection with regard to impurity and purity). That is, if a Jew poured wine from his bottle into a gentile's cup in which the wine is forbidden for benefit, since the poured wine connects the wine in the two vessels, all the wine in the bottle becomes forbidden. Some say that the halakha does not follow Rav Huna, and the wine in the bottle is permitted (Rabbeinu Tam, Riaz, Semag, and so it is cited in the name of several Geonim). However, according to most Rishonim the halakha follows Rav Huna, and the wine in the bottle is forbidden by the law of nitzok (Rabbeinu Ḥananel, Rif, Rashi, Rashbam, Rambam, Ra'avad, Ra'avan, Rashba, Meiri, Rabbeinu Yeruḥam, Ran and others). And some decided to be stringent ab initio regarding the law of nitzok and to be lenient after the fact (Razah and Ritva). And some are lenient only in a case of great loss (Tosafot, Maharam, Rosh and Mordekhai). And so it was ruled in SA and Rema 126:1-2.<br>Some say that the whole stringency of nitzok applies only when the wine in the cup became forbidden because of touch, but if it became forbidden only because of his force, for example by a gentile having poured it, it is not able to forbid the nitzok (Rashi, Avoda Zara 72b, according to the opinion of Rav Sheshet; Rashbam, Ra'avan, Tosafot, Rosh and others). And some hold that even wine that became forbidden because of his force forbids the nitzok (Rif, Rambam, Ra'avad, Ramban, Rashba and others). And so it was ruled in SA 125:1.<br>They further disagreed regarding the opinion of Rav Huna: some hold that if the wine in the cup is forbidden for benefit because of the gentile's touch, the nitzok in the bottle too becomes forbidden for benefit (Ra'avad), and some hold that it becomes forbidden only for drinking (Rashba), and this is the accepted halakha (Beit Yosef 126:5; Beit Meir 1; Erekh Ha-shulḥan 1). As a rule, the nitzok is forbidden one degree less; therefore, if a gentile opened the tap of a barrel, the wine that poured out by his force is forbidden for drinking, and what remained is permitted (SA 124:14), except for cases in which, for additional reasons, one is stringent to regard it at the same degree as the case where a gentile pours into a cup (see SA 125:1; Yabi'a Omer YD vol. 1, 11:21).<br>Even when the gentile tilted the barrel without lifting it, the wine that poured out is considered to be by his force (Taz, Shakh, Knesset Ha-gedola, Gra), and some say that it is not considered his force and is permitted (Rema 125:1). Pumping wine by means of an electric machine is also considered the gentile's force in all its laws (Har Tzvi YD 111).<br>If he poured from the bottle without intention, or he thought there was beer in it and not wine, even what came out is permitted for drinking, for his force without intention is permitted (SA 125:1). If a gentile poured water into a Jew's wine in order to dilute it, the wine is forbidden for drinking (SA 125:7)."},{"n":10,"en":"According to the lenient opinion, since it was ruled in the Rambam (Hilkhot Ma'akhalot Asurot 13:11) and SA 124:7 that the law of a gentile who does not engage in idolatry goes down a degree in its stringency, since the force of an idolater forbids for drinking, consequently when he does not engage in idolatry it does not forbid at all, and consequently there is no prohibition of nitzok with him. And so wrote the Shakh 124:11; Dagul Me-revava and Rabbi Akiva Eiger in their glosses on the Taz 124:32; Erekh Ha-shulḥan 126:10; Simḥat Kohen YD 1:179; Ish Matzli'aḥ YD 1:12:1; Ḥazon Ish YD 49:7.<br>On the other hand, some hold that since according to many (above, at the beginning of note 9) his force forbids for benefit, one should be stringent with one who does not engage in idolatry and forbid what he poured for drinking. But the wine that remained in the bottle does not become forbidden because of nitzok, according to the opinion of those who hold that its law is more lenient than the law of the wine poured by his force (as explained above in note 9). And so wrote in practice the Taz 124:32, at its end; Beit Yehuda (Ayash) 2:116; Tiferet Adam YD 34:1; Yabi'a Omer YD 1:11. And although according to the rules of halakha, as explained in note 7, one should rule completely according to the lenient opinion, many are accustomed to forbid for drinking wine that a gentile who does not engage in idolatry poured by his force, as cited in Shulḥan Shlomo (Rav Shlomo Zalman Auerbach) 272, note 6. And so wrote the Tzitz Eliezer 12:52 regarding one who publicly desecrates Shabbat. Not only that, but in their view the custom is to be stringent even regarding what remained in the bottle, lest the gentile touched the wine that he poured, and lest he agitated the bottle slightly, and they are concerned for the opinion of individual authorities (Yismaḥ Lev YD 14) who hold that even slight agitating forbids.<br>The Shakh (125:1) was lenient in a case of loss even regarding wine that an idolater poured, since the gentiles in our times are not considered idolaters. Thus far. His words are directed at the Christians in whose lands the Jews of Ashkenaz lived. And it is possible that he means that they are not accustomed to pour libations to idolatry. And the Igrot Moshe (YD 2:51) agreed with the Shakh's permission, but wrote that his entire permission is after the fact, incidentally, but to arrange this ab initio is forbidden, and even in a case of loss one should not be lenient in this, especially since there is reason to be concerned that perhaps he will touch the wine. And likewise the custom is to be stringent regarding Muslim waiters, because of the concern that they may touch the wine and forbid it for drinking.<br>After the fact, when a gentile who does not engage in idolatry poured from the wine, the wine is kosher, as explained above according to most poskim. And one need not be concerned, without a special reason, that the gentile agitated the wine inside the bottle or touched it. Nor need one be concerned that perhaps he is an idolater, for we learned that in a case of loss the Shakh 125:1 was lenient. And since clarifying whether the waiter is an idolater involves unpleasantness and a concern of insult, this is like a case of loss, and the wine should be permitted. And if it is known that the waiter is a Christian who believes that \"that man\" is God, in a case of loss the wine is permitted, and not in a case of loss, the wine he poured and also the wine that remained in the bottle are forbidden for drinking and permitted for benefit. And if it is known that the waiter is an actual idol worshipper, even in a case of loss the wine he poured and the wine that remained in the bottle are forbidden for drinking and permitted for benefit.<br>With a Jew who publicly desecrates Shabbat, one may be lenient regarding pouring even ab initio (below, halakha 13)."}]},{"slug":"17-29-09","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/17-29-09/","title_he":"ט – שמירת היין","title_en":"Guarding the Wine","body_en":"<p>When a Jew wishes to deposit his wine with an idolatrous gentile, he must place the wine closed in a vessel with two seals. As long as the vessel remains closed with the two seals – the wine in it is kosher, and if the seals were opened – the wine is forbidden. When the gentile does not engage in idolatry, one seal is sufficient. A seal means a special closure that is difficult to forge. For example, one can stick adhesive tape over the opening of the bottle and sign across the place of attachment, so that if the tape is opened, it will be difficult to restore the signature to its original form. And if he sticks another piece of tape over the opening and signs on it too, it will be considered two seals. A seal can also be a special knot of a plastic bag, which it is difficult for one who opens the bottle to restore to its exact form. In order to seal the bottle with two seals, factories are accustomed to seal wine bottles with a cork or metal stopper, and over this they stick another plastic wrapping.</p><p>If an idolatrous gentile stayed alone with a bottle of wine that is not sealed with two seals, the wine is forbidden for benefit, even if he stayed with it for a short time, for since the gentiles were accustomed to pour wine as libations, the Sages decreed that any wine that a gentile could have poured as a libation would be forbidden as though he had poured it. But if the gentile does not engage in idolatry, there is no concern that he will want to pour it as a libation. However, if he stayed alone with the wine, and there is a concern that he will drink from the wine directly from the mouth of the bottle or touch the wine inside it – the wine is forbidden for drinking, and only if it was sealed with one seal is it not forbidden. And if there is no concern that he will drink from the wine or touch it – the wine is kosher even without a seal (SA 128:1; Rema 4; Shakh 1). According to many, the law of an idolatrous gentile who is not accustomed to pour wine as a libation is like the law of a gentile who does not engage in idolatry.<sup>11</sup></p><p>If they warned the gentile not to touch the wine bottles at all, and if he violates their warning he will be considered a thief who may be punished for it, for example his terms of employment may be harmed – even if they left him alone in the house and the bottle of wine is not sealed, as long as there is a possibility that they will suddenly come in and see him touching the wine, or see him through a window or by means of a closed-circuit camera, the wine is permitted. But if there was a time when the gentile knew that there was no chance that they would see him, since he could then have drunk from the wine without being caught as a thief – the wine is forbidden (SA 128:4; 129:1). Therefore, one who employs a gentile in his home, and there are times when the gentile is alone in the house and might drink from the wine, must take care that the wine bottles are sealed properly, or lock them in a cupboard that the gentile has no possibility of managing to open. And if he did not do so, the wine is forbidden. And if the wine is cooked or sweetened, it does not have the law of yayin nesekh, and it need not be guarded.<sup>12</sup></p>","notes_en":[{"n":11,"en":"It is explained in the Gemara (Avoda Zara 39a-b) that one who deposits wine with an idolatrous gentile must seal it with two seals. And if it was sealed with one seal, it is forbidden for drinking and permitted for benefit (ibid. 31a; SA 130:1-2). If the seals were opened – the wine becomes forbidden (ibid. 130:8). Two seals are required, as is the guarding required for a Torah prohibition, even though their ordinary wine is forbidden rabbinically, and for a rabbinic prohibition one seal is sufficient (Rambam, Rashba and SA 118:1), because of the concern lest he pour the wine as a libation and it become forbidden by Torah law (Rashba, cited in Beit Yosef 118:1). With cooked wine there is no concern of libation, but there is a concern that the gentile may exchange it for his own wine, and for this one seal is sufficient (Avoda Zara 29b; SA 130:3). Likewise, when depositing wine with a gentile who does not engage in idolatry, one seal is required (Shakh 118:4; Kaf Ha-ḥayim 118:5).<br>Many hold that since today idolaters are not accustomed to pour libations, one seal is sufficient (Eshkol in the name of Rav Hai Gaon, Or Zarua, Roke'aḥ, Taz 118:4; Arukh Ha-shulḥan 13; Tzitz Eliezer 12:56; and others). And those who hold that the touch of idolatrous gentiles does not forbid for benefit today, since they are not accustomed to pour libations (above, note 3), will agree to this as well. In addition, what we learned, that with a Torah prohibition and their ordinary wine two seals are required, is according to the opinion of the Rambam, the Rashba and those who agree with them, which was cited as the unattributed opinion in SA 118:1. But the SA wrote a \"some say\" opinion, which is the opinion of Tosafot, that the entire law of two seals applies when one sends the food and the Jew does not check his seal, but when he checks his seal, one seal is sufficient. And the Rema also cited the opinion of Rabbeinu Tam, that only when depositing with a Jewish apostate are two seals required, but when depositing with a gentile, one seal is always sufficient, and the Rema wrote that after the fact one may rely on him (and see below 38:7).<br>A metal cap that, after it has been opened for the first time, cannot be restored to its original state without the help of a machine is effective as two seals, and therefore one may deposit a bottle of wine closed with a metal cap with an idolatrous gentile. And so it is cited in Yein Malkhut 12:15 in the name of Rav Nissim Karelitz. Admittedly, one could argue that in any case the Sages instituted two seals. However, since some hold that one seal is sufficient, one may rely on this simple reasoning. But regarding a cork stopper, where it is possible with difficulty to restore it to how it was, it should be regarded as one seal."},{"n":12,"en":"When there is a concern that a gentile who does not engage in idolatry will want to exchange the Jew's fine wine for his own ordinary wine, if he stayed alone with the wine for twenty minutes (more than the measure of walking a mil), the wine is forbidden for drinking (SA 128:1). At the end of a wedding, waiters sometimes collect the wine; if they warned them not to drink from it, and if they drink they will be caught as thieves and punished, and there is a Jew there who is likely to see them if they violate the warning – the wine is permitted. For according to halakha, carrying the bottles and pouring from them does not forbid the wine, as we learned in halakha 8."}]},{"slug":"17-29-10","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/17-29-10/","title_he":"י – דינים נוספים","title_en":"Additional Laws","body_en":"<p>In order to distance Israel from the prohibition, the Sages instructed that ab initio, when gentiles harvest grapes for a Jew, care should be taken to place the grapes in vessels that have holes, so that liquids that come out of the grapes will not collect at the bottom of the vessel. But after the fact, if they harvested into vessels and liquids collected at the bottom of the vessel and the gentile touched them, they are not forbidden, since they had not yet been separated from the grapes. Similarly, ab initio one should not let a gentile carry grapes to the winepress, and one should not let him pour the grapes into the winepress, lest he touch the wine in the winepress; and after the fact, if he threw them into the winepress, he did not forbid the winepress (Avoda Zara 59b; SA 125:6). And some wrote that even with a mechanical harvester, ab initio a Jew should operate it, since during the harvest liquids squeezed from the grapes collect in the harvester's container. However, after the fact, even in their opinion there is no prohibition in this, since these liquids have not been separated from the seeds and skins (Rav Eliyahu; Bein Yisrael La-amim 21:3).</p><p>Everything the Sages decreed regarding wine that a gentile touched applies when the gentile did not intend to provoke the Jew and cause him to lose his wine; but if he touched the wine in order to harm the Jew and forbid his wine, the Sages did not decree, and the wine is permitted for drinking. On the contrary, the Jew should make an effort to drink the wine in front of the gentile, so that he will know that he did not succeed by his actions in disqualifying the wine (Tosafot Avoda Zara 58b, s.v. \"Katav\"; Beit Yosef 132:1; Rema 124:27).</p>","notes_en":[]},{"slug":"17-29-11","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/17-29-11/","title_he":"יא – משקאות אלכוהוליים","title_en":"Alcoholic Beverages","body_en":"<p>After the Sages forbade the wine, bread and cooking of gentiles, the Amoraim added a further safeguard against assimilation, that a Jew should not drink an alcoholic beverage in a gentile's home, or in his shop, or in his café, or in any place in his domain (Avoda Zara 31b). Included in the prohibition are all alcoholic beverages, such as whiskey, arak, vodka, liqueur, beer and the like. The prohibition applies even when the Jew drinks the beverage alone, without company with the gentile, since the drink loosens a person and may bring down the dignified barrier that should fence in Israel. There was no need to decree this in the days of the Tanna'im in the Land of Israel, because their drinking was mainly wine, and regarding it they established severe decrees; but in Babylonia, where people were accustomed to drink alcoholic beverages of other kinds, they needed to forbid these beverages as well (Arukh Ha-shulḥan 114:6).</p><p>There is a difference between the decree on the bread and cooked dishes of gentiles and the prohibition of drinking alcoholic beverages at a gentile's: the prohibition of eating the bread and cooked dishes of gentiles applies to the bread and cooked dish prepared by gentiles, and it is forbidden to eat them even in a Jewish home. But regarding a beverage, the prohibition is not on the beverage but on drinking it at the gentile's. Therefore, even if a Jew prepared a beverage and brought it himself to the gentile's home, he is forbidden to drink it in the gentile's home, even if he drinks it alone. On the other hand, not in the gentile's home, a Jew is permitted to drink a beverage that a gentile prepared from kosher ingredients (SA 114:1). And it is not subject to the prohibition of bishulei goyim, since it consists mainly of water (above 28:7). That is, the aim of the prohibition of drinking alcoholic beverages at a gentile's is to prevent the closeness that may develop while drinking.<sup>13</sup></p><p>However, on a temporary basis for a great need it is permitted. For example, when a Jew needs to drink a little alcoholic beverage in order to strengthen himself or to prevent headaches, he is permitted to enter a gentile's bar, buy one glass, drink it and leave. Likewise, if he happened to be in a gentile's home for another purpose and they offered him a drink, and he especially needs it, he is permitted to drink one glass, on condition that they do not sit together to drink (SA 114:1). And if he has already been compelled to drink twice in a gentile's bar or home, even if he will need it, he should not drink with them a third time, for he has already gone from temporary drinking to regular drinking, and there is concern that this will lead to excessive closeness (Knesset Ha-gedola; Pri Ḥadash 4; Kaf Ha-ḥayim 6).</p><p>One who has to travel from his home to another place and is lodging in a gentile's home, whether for payment or for free, may bring a drink with him and drink it in the gentile's home, for at that time the gentile's home is considered his home. But he should not buy a drink from the host and drink it there. And if the host offers him a little drink, so that enmity will not arise between them, he is permitted to accept and drink a little (Tosafot Avoda Zara 31b, s.v. \"Ve-tarvayhu\"; SA 114:1; Shakh 2). Only with a beverage were they lenient to drink on a temporary basis in order to prevent enmity, but there is no permission to eat the bread and cooked dishes of gentiles in order to prevent enmity (Taz 152:1).</p><p>Regarding drinking coffee and tea, the poskim disagreed. Some say that since these too are customarily drunk in company and drinking them draws hearts close, it is forbidden to drink them in a gentile's home (Radbaz, the Arizal, Ḥokhmat Adam 66:14). And some say that there is no prohibition to drink them in a gentile's home, since the prohibition applies only to an alcoholic beverage. This is on condition that their sitting there does not involve frivolity (Pri Ḥadash 114:6; Ḥatam Sofer, Avoda Zara 31b; Ben Ish Ḥai, Year Two, Ḥukat 16). In practice, as a matter of friendship – it is proper to be stringent; and as a matter of respect for the host – one may be lenient.</p>","notes_en":[{"n":13,"en":"After the Sages decreed on wine, bread and cooked dishes, the Amoraim added a prohibition on the beer of gentiles because of intermarriage, as cited in Avoda Zara 31b. According to the Rambam, Tosafot and Rosh, the prohibition was established as a decree; according to the Rashba and Meiri, as a custom that was accepted and is binding. There in the Gemara it is related that Rav Papa would buy the beer in the gentile's shop, go out the door of the shop and drink it there, and Rav Aḥai practiced an additional distancing and would drink it in his own home. Some were lenient in practice like Rav Papa (Semag and Rabbeinu Yeruḥam). However, the Rambam (Hilkhot Ma'akhalot Asurot 17:10), Radbaz and SA 114:1 were stringent like Rav Aḥai. But I did not write as a condition that he go to his home, for it seems that even the stringent ones agree that if he goes to another place, such as a nearby garden or his friend's house, he has already distanced himself from the gentile's home and it is permitted. And so wrote the Shulḥan Gavoha 114:6 and Kaf Ha-ḥayim 5.<br>With the bread and cooked dishes of gentiles they were more stringent and forbade eating them even in a Jewish home, because they sustain life and the concern of closeness with them is deeper and more continuous (Rosh). Moreover, women often prepare them, and the concern of intermarriage is greater (Ran). Moreover, the prohibition of beverages was established in the days of the Amoraim, and they did not want to equate it with the stringency of the decree of the Tanna'im (Ra'avan, Ramban).<br>In the Gemara (Avoda Zara 31b) the prohibition is mentioned regarding date beer, but since it is because of the concern of intermarriage and not because of a particular problem that existed with date beer, it applies to every alcoholic beverage. And so it was ruled in the SA, based on Tosafot 31b, s.v. \"Tarvayhu,\" Teruma, Semag, Agudah. And so wrote the Pri Ḥadash, Gra and many other Aḥaronim. However, some hold that the prohibition applies only to date beer (Mordekhai). And the Rema wrote (114:1) that on this they relied in Ashkenaz to be lenient and drink \"mead\" (<i>mead</i>), which is a light intoxicating drink made from grain and honey (up to about 5% alcohol, like beer). And from some it is implied that they were lenient even with beverages with more alcohol. Apparently, many in Ashkenaz became accustomed to and needed an alcoholic beverage every day, by which they warmed themselves in the cold days, to the point that such routine drinking was not considered by them to be drinking that gladdens and draws hearts close; and on this basis the Arukh Ha-shulḥan wrote (114:11) that one may be lenient with simple beverages. However, from the Darkhei Moshe 1 it appears that even with a beverage that has a little alcohol the permission is strained, but this was the custom. And so wrote the Baḥ and Ḥokhmat Adam 66:14, that although one should not protest against those who are lenient, it is proper to be stringent. And since even for many of the Ashkenazi poskim the permission is strained and was intended for a public that needed it and was accustomed to it, and even among the lenient ones some were lenient only with a beverage with a little alcohol, I did not cite this lenient opinion above, for in the Land of Israel in our times people are not accustomed to need this beverage. In addition, it is difficult to distinguish between more important and less important kinds of beer, and likewise it is difficult to set a limit of alcohol percentage beyond which it is forbidden. Therefore it is proper to follow the majority of poskim, who hold that every alcoholic beverage is included in the prohibition. And although the Rambam (Hilkhot Ma'akhalot Asurot 17:11) and SA 114:3 wrote that the prohibition does not apply to apple wine and pomegranate wine because they are not common, today it seems that people are accustomed to prepare an intoxicating beverage from every fruit and plant, to the point that the rule is that everything is common, and the prohibition applies to every alcoholic beverage. Similarly wrote the Shevet Ha-levi 2:43."}]},{"slug":"17-29-12","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/17-29-12/","title_he":"יב – מסיבה של גויים","title_en":"A Party of Gentiles","body_en":"<p>At a gathering of gentiles for raising a toast, a Jew is forbidden to drink even a little alcoholic beverage, even if the drinking takes place in a public hall that does not belong to the gentile, and even if the Jew brought the beverage from his home. But there is no prohibition to eat from the light refreshments served there, on condition that they are kosher. And if it is a gathering of Jews, even if there were gentiles at it, since most of the participants are Jews – it is permitted even to drink an alcoholic beverage (based on Rambam, Hilkhot Ma'akhalot Asurot 17:10).</p><p>At a party of gentiles, since the meal is more important, not only a beverage is forbidden, but even kosher food may not be eaten there. Even if the gentile host set aside a table for the Jews on which mehadrin kosher food is served, it is forbidden to eat there. Included in a party are: a wedding, a party in honor of a birth, and all the more so a meal in honor of a holiday, such as Christmas, New Year's Eve (Sylvester) and Eid al-Adha (the Festival of the Sacrifice). But for an ordinary meal without special festivity, it is permitted to be a guest in a gentile's home or to dine with him in a restaurant, on condition that the food is kosher and the Jew is careful not to drink alcohol (Levush; Ḥokhmat Adam 87:12).</p><p>The prohibition of dining at a party of gentiles too is intended to serve as a barrier against assimilation. And so we learned that the Torah warned against participating in the meals of gentiles because of the concern of assimilation, as it is stated (Shemot 34:15-16): \"Lest you make a covenant with the inhabitants of the land … and one call you and you eat of his sacrifice (his meal); and you take of his daughters for your sons, and his daughters go astray after their gods and make your sons go astray after their gods.\" And when it is a party of an idolatrous gentile, participating in his meal also involves supporting his religion. And concerning this the Sages said (Avoda Zara 8a): \"Jews outside the Land are idol worshippers in purity (that is, influenced by the culture of their religion). How so? A gentile who made a feast for his son and invited all the Jews in his city – even though they eat their own food and drink their own drink and their own attendant stands before them, Scripture regards them as though they ate of the sacrifices to the dead\" (sacrifices of idolatry). And likewise the Sages said that the decree in the days of Haman was decreed on Israel because they enjoyed the feast of Aḥashverosh (see Megilla 12a; Yalkut Shimoni, Esther 1056).</p><p>However, the foods that were served at the party are not forbidden, and therefore if the gentile sent some of them to a Jew's home, and it is clear that they are kosher without question, the Jew is permitted to eat them (SA YD 152:1-2).</p><p>When there is a concern that refraining from participating in a wedding or a party will cause enmity, the poskim disagreed whether it is permitted to participate in the party and taste the kosher foods. Some say that it is forbidden, since the main concern is lest they come to intermarriage and assimilation, and consequently the concern of enmity does not cancel this; on the contrary, it is good that distance be created between them (Taz 152:1). And some say that it is permitted, and there is no need to be concerned about cultural influence, since the participation is only because of enmity (see Nekudot Ha-kesef there). In practice, the accepted custom is to try to avoid it as much as possible, and to give a nice gift and heartfelt good wishes instead of participating in the party. And when there is no possibility of avoiding it, one relies on the lenient opinions and participates to the small extent required to prevent enmity and maintain good relations. In any case, all alcoholic drinking is forbidden.<sup>14</sup></p>","notes_en":[{"n":14,"en":"Avoda Zara 8a-b: \"It was taught: Rabbi Yishmael says: Jews outside the Land are idol worshippers in purity. How so? An idol worshipper who made a feast for his son (for his wedding) and invited all the Jews in his city, even though they eat their own food and drink their own drink and their own attendant stands before them, Scripture regards them as though they ate of the sacrifices to the dead … (even though they ate kosher food).\" Throughout the thirty days from the wedding, it is forbidden to dine at his home even if it was not said that the meal is on account of the wedding; afterwards, until twelve months, the prohibition applies only if it was said that the meal is in honor of the wedding. And it is explained in the Gemara there that the reason is that the gentile gives thanks to his idols for the guest who participated in his meal. And so wrote the Rambam (Hilkhot Avoda Zara 9:15), the Rashba and the Meiri on Avoda Zara 8a. Based on their words, the Shakh wrote in Nekudot Ha-kesef (152:1) that the prohibition applies specifically to an idolatrous gentile. On the other hand, the Rosh (Responsa, Kelal 19:22) and the Radbaz (4:21) wrote that the prohibition applies also to a gentile who does not engage in idolatry. And the Taz explained (152:1) that the prohibition is because of intermarriage and distancing, and therefore it applies to all gentiles, for it is forbidden to intermarry with all gentiles. And it seems that the basis of the prohibition is not only the concern of marriage, but cultural influence that may lead to assimilation, as is the implication of the verse (Shemot 34:15-16). Consequently, it seems that even the Rambam and those who agree with him would agree that the prohibition applies to all gentiles, since the concern of cultural influence applies to all gentile culture. And this is the reason the Rambam quoted the entire verse, which includes the concern of marriage (and so wrote Mishneh Halakhot 7:118). And so it emerges from the words of the Sages in Tanna De-vei Eliyahu Rabba (chapters 10, 21), that the prohibition of participating in a meal of gentiles is because of intermarriage and assimilation. And it is obvious that this is the law regarding every important party, such as a party in honor of a birth (Responsa Ḥesed Le-Avraham, Alkalai, 26). All the more so a party in honor of gentile holidays, such as Christmas, New Year's Eve and Eid al-Adha. This prohibition is by Torah law according to most poskim, and so wrote the Rosh, Ritva, Yam Shel Shlomo, Taz, Erekh Ha-shulḥan, Netziv. And according to the Shakh the prohibition is rabbinic and the verse is an asmakhta (a supporting allusion), and several Aḥaronim added his opinion in weighing the halakha.<br>The Shakh wrote in Nekudot Ha-kesef 152:1 that in a case of enmity one may participate even in the meal of an idolater. And so wrote the Tzvi La-tzaddik, Igrot Moshe YD 2:117, and others. On the other hand, according to the Taz, the concern of enmity does not permit it, for the whole purpose is to create distance. And so wrote the Ritva (Avoda Zara 8a), Ḥesed La-alafim 152:1; Maharsham there; Rabbi Ḥayim Palagi in Ḥayim Be-yad 29; Halikhot Olam vol. 7, Mas'ei 7; and others. However, it seems that in a time of pressing need, when there is no possibility of avoiding it, one may rely on the Shakh and those who agree with him, and participate in the party to the small extent sufficient to prevent enmity, for in this manner there is less concern of cultural influence."}]},{"slug":"17-29-13","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/17-29-13/","title_he":"יג – יהודי מחלל שבת","title_en":"A Jew Who Desecrates Shabbat","body_en":"<p>The attitude toward desecration of Shabbat is especially severe, to the point that the Sages said that one may not accept a sacrifice from a Jew who publicly desecrates Shabbat, his slaughtering is invalid, and he invalidates the eruv like a gentile (Ḥullin 5a; Eruvin 69b). Based on this, the Rambam wrote (Hilkhot Shabbat 30:15): \"Shabbat and idolatry – each of the two is equal to all the other mitzvot of the Torah. And Shabbat is the sign between the Holy One, blessed be He, and us forever. Therefore, whoever transgresses the other mitzvot is included among the wicked of Israel, but one who publicly desecrates Shabbat is like an idolater, and both are like gentiles in all respects.\" Similarly, several Rishonim wrote that wine that a Jew who publicly desecrates Shabbat touched is forbidden like wine that a gentile who does not engage in idolatry touched, which is forbidden for drinking and permitted for benefit (Behag, Rabbeinu Yona, Eshkol, Rivash and others).</p><p>And although many Rishonim did not mention this law, the Aḥaronim agreed in halakha that wine that one who publicly desecrates Shabbat touched is forbidden for drinking. And although the prohibition of wine that a gentile touched is because of the concern of assimilation, which is not relevant with regard to Jews who desecrate Shabbat, since for several laws they equated one who desecrates Shabbat with a gentile, they applied the stringency of this law to them as well. Moreover, they forbade their touch as a penalty and a protest against those who transgress such a severe transgression. For Shabbat was the most distinct expression of Jewish identity, and whoever dared to desecrate Shabbat publicly thereby committed a terrible act of defiance against the faith of Israel, and announced before the whole world that he does not identify with Judaism.</p><p>However, only if he touched the wine itself or drank from it does the wine become forbidden; but if he poured from the bottle into the cup, the wine does not become forbidden, for this is the law according to most poskim even with a gentile who does not engage in idolatry. Admittedly, some are stringent regarding a gentile, but regarding a Jew there is no room to be stringent (above, 8). All the more so if he moved the cup from place to place, the wine does not become forbidden, for even an idolatrous gentile does not forbid the wine in this manner (above, 7).</p><p>In recent generations the question of the attitude toward those who publicly desecrate Shabbat has arisen anew. For, to our great sorrow, many Jews began to desecrate Shabbat, until a situation arose in which Shabbat is no longer the expression that characterizes Jewish identity. Not only that, but whereas in the past the protest against Shabbat desecrators was effective in preventing them from breaching the fence, in recent generations the protest has generally distanced more than it has fenced in. Therefore many of the later poskim were lenient in a time of need, and ruled that only one who desecrates Shabbat demonstratively, in order to provoke and defy the tradition, is considered like a gentile, but one who honors Shabbat with Kiddush and lighting candles is not considered like a gentile. Moreover, if he is a Jew who did not merit a Torah education, he is like one acting under duress, since he does not understand the severity of desecrating Shabbat. And even if he grew up in a religious home, sometimes the secular influence is so strong that he is close to being considered one who acts unwittingly and under duress, who cannot withstand the spirit of the times, and he should not be regarded as a gentile (Binyan Tziyon Ḥadashot 23; Melamed Le-ho'il OḤ 29; Igrot Ha-Re'aya vol. 1, letter 138). Moreover, stringency in these distancings may cause insults and disputes in families and communities (Rav Yosef Mashash).</p><p>And although some are stringent even in our time, with the aim of preventing the influence of Shabbat desecrators on those who observe the mitzvot, it is agreed in practice by most poskim that in a time of need, in order to prevent insult or harm to the brotherhood that should exist among Israel, one should not be stringent regarding wine that Shabbat desecrators touched, as long as they do not do so to provoke. And likewise, if he wishes, he is counted in a minyan (Peninei Halakha: Tefilla 2:8).<sup>15</sup></p>","notes_en":[{"n":15,"en":"Some hold that the law of one who publicly desecrates Shabbat is like an actual idolater, whose wine is forbidden for benefit (Tevu'ot Shor 11:12, based on Behag and Rashba). On the other hand, some hold that one who desecrates Shabbat is not considered a gentile with regard to wine, since this law is not written explicitly in the Gemara, and most Rishonim did not mention it; and so wrote Rashi (Responsa Rashi 169), Rabbi Ḥayim ben Attar in Ḥefetz Hashem, Ḥullin 4b, and Yad Yehuda. However, the overwhelming majority of the Aḥaronim hold that the law of one who publicly desecrates Shabbat is like a gentile who does not engage in idolatry, who forbids the wine for drinking but not for benefit. And although there is no explicit source for this law in the Gemara, it is a custom founded on the enactment of the Sages, who established that the law of one who publicly desecrates Shabbat is like one who is an apostate with regard to the entire Torah with respect to sacrifices, eruvin and slaughter (Igrot Moshe OḤ 5:37). And some say that it is because of a penalty (Ḥatam Sofer YD 120; Beit She'arim YD 220, s.v. \"Aval mah she-katav\"), and it seems that the penalty was intended as a protest and a distancing from those who publicly desecrate Shabbat, so that they would repent and not have a bad influence.<br>However, regarding Shabbat desecrators in recent generations there is a disagreement. Among those who are stringent even in our time: Pri Ha-sadeh 1:62; Minḥat Yeḥiel 1:105; Minḥat Elazar 1:74; Yaskil Avdi vol. 8, YD 19; Tzitz Eliezer 12:56. However, the opinion of most poskim is that in a time of need one may be lenient, and the more the person honors the tradition, the broader the permission. And conversely, the more the Shabbat desecrator knows the severity of the prohibition and the less he honors the tradition, the narrower the permission. But after all, the general opinion is that in order to prevent insult and to preserve the proper brotherhood among Israel, one may be lenient in a time of need. So wrote Responsa Yehuda Ya'aleh YD 50; Binyan Tziyon Ḥadashot 23; Ori Ve-yish'i 100; Melamed Le-ho'il OḤ 29, YD 52; Maharsham 1:121; Levushei Mordekhai; Aḥi'ezer 4:37; Sho'el U-nish'al 3:216; Ḥelkat Ya'akov YD 48; Rav Yosef Mashash in Otzar Ha-mikhtavim vol. 2, 1302; Beit David 1:132; Yabi'a Omer vol. 1, YD 11; Le-horot Natan 1:39; Aseh Lekha Rav 2:51; and many others.<br>It seems to me, in my humble opinion, that when engaged in a mitzva, such as at a Shabbat meal conducted according to halakha, a ḥuppa conducted according to halakha, and likewise a wedding meal conducted according to halakha without mixed dancing, one may ab initio regard everyone who at that time honors the tradition as proper Jews. This is because their gathering there is for the sake of a mitzva, and as the Sages said (Mishna Ḥagiga 3:6) regarding amei ha-aretz (the unlearned), that at the time they go up to Jerusalem for the pilgrimage festival they are considered ḥaverim (trustworthy associates): \"In Jerusalem they are trusted regarding sacred food, and at the time of the festival also regarding teruma.\" And the reason given in the Gemara (26a): \"For the verse states (Shoftim 20:11): 'So all the men of Israel were gathered against the city, united as one man, ḥaverim' – Scripture made them all ḥaverim.\""}]},{"slug":"17-29-14","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/17-29-14/","title_he":"יד – איסור שתייה בקלות ראש","title_en":"The Prohibition of Drinking Frivolously","body_en":"<p>One should not drink an alcoholic beverage out of frivolity or in the company of frivolous people, because the influence of alcohol is great, and therefore the Sages instructed to be very careful with it. As we learned in the Torah, even a perfectly righteous man like Noaḥ, when he was not careful with wine, stumbled and fell into the depths of shame and disgrace. Likewise Lot, through drinking, came to illicit relations with his daughters (Bereishit Rabba 36:4). Likewise Nadav and Avihu, the sons of Aharon the Kohen, who were righteous like their father and were supposed to inherit his place, since they were not careful with wine and entered the Mishkan intoxicated, were punished and died (Vayikra Rabba 12:1). And the Sages said (Sanhedrin 70a): \"There is nothing that brings wailing upon a person except wine.\" They further said that wine may bring a person to transgression and illicit relations, and therefore one who fears that his inclination will overpower him should make himself a nazir, abstaining from wine (Nazir 2a). When they said \"wine,\" they meant alcohol, for in their time alcohol was drunk mainly as wine.</p><p>When alcohol is drunk in a group with music, the concern greatly increases that a person will become wild, forget his purpose and be drawn after his desires. Many evils, including the exile, came upon Israel as a result of this. As it is stated (Yeshayahu 5:11-15): \"Woe to those who rise early in the morning to pursue strong drink, who tarry late into the night till wine inflames them. And the lyre and the harp, the timbrel and the flute, and wine are at their feasts; but they do not regard the work of the Lord, nor have they seen the work of His hands. Therefore My people have gone into exile for lack of knowledge, and their honored men are famished (its dignitaries will die of hunger), and their multitude is parched with thirst (in place of the wine they drank). Therefore Sheol has enlarged its appetite and opened its mouth without measure (without limit), and her splendor and her multitude and her tumult, and he who exults in her, go down into it. And man is bowed down, and man is humbled, and the eyes of the haughty are humbled\" (Sota 48a).</p><p>However, wine also has a good side, in that it can give expression to true joy (above, 1). As it is stated in the chapter of thanksgiving to God, that among the good things God gave man He also gave wine, as it is stated (Tehillim 104:1-15): \"Bless the Lord, O my soul … and wine that gladdens the heart of man.\" And the Sages said (Berakhot 35a): \"Song is recited only over wine.\" Therefore they instituted reciting Kiddush over a cup of wine on Shabbatot and festivals, at weddings and britot. And as stated in the Zohar (vol. 3, 189b): \"There is no sanctification except with wine, and there is no blessing except with wine.\"</p><p>Thus the distinction is between joy and drinking of a mitzva and joy and drinking of the mundane, as the Sages said (Shabbat 30b) that the proper joy is the joy of a mitzva, as it is stated (Kohelet 8:15): \"So I commended joy, for a man has no better thing under the sun than to eat, and to drink, and to be joyful, and that this should accompany him in his labor all the days of his life which God has given him under the sun.\" But one must beware of joy that is not of a mitzva, as it is stated (ibid. 2:2): \"I said of laughter, 'It is mad,' and of joy, 'What does it accomplish?'\", for this laughter is \"mehulal,\" that is, mixed with weeping and sighing, and this joy ends in sorrow (Rashi there). And when mundane drinking is done in a group, it is more disgraceful and is considered \"a seat of scorners,\" as it is stated (Mishlei 20:1): \"Wine is a mocker, strong drink is riotous, and whoever is led astray by it is not wise.\" Rabbeinu Yona explained (Sha'arei Teshuva 3:177): \"Drinking wine causes three bad things: a) that the man becomes a scoffer; b) that he becomes riotous and a man of many words, and our Sages of blessed memory said (Avot 1:17): 'And whoever multiplies words brings sin…'; c) 'and whoever is led astray by it is not wise.'\"</p><p>And likewise the Sages ruled in halakha that it is forbidden to sing or play musical instruments while drinking wine, and only for songs and praises to God, or when engaged in a mitzva meal such as a wedding, is it permitted to sing and play (SA OḤ 560:3).<sup>16</sup></p><p>Therefore, one should not drink in clubs and at parties of friends in a secular atmosphere, for one who is drawn after such drinking may become distanced from Torah and mitzvot and come to transgression. And this is what the Sages meant: \"Keep far from a bad neighbor, and do not associate with the wicked\" (Avot 1:7). Admittedly, it is possible that those who drink frivolously in pubs and clubs conduct themselves like righteous people in other areas, and then it is proper to create cooperation and friendships with them. But in a place where they are accustomed to drink frivolously, the bad aspects of casting off Torah and mitzvot come to expression, and at that time they are considered bad company who may draw one away from the ways of Torah and mitzvot. And concerning this it is stated (Tehillim 1:1): \"Happy is the man who has not walked in the counsel of the wicked, nor stood in the way of sinners, nor sat in the seat of scorners.\" The Sages explained (Avoda Zara 18b) that a person should not say, \"I will go only once to a feast of scorners,\" for if he goes – he will end up standing there, and if he stands – he will end up sitting there and becoming a scorner.</p><p>Sometimes a person is invited by his workplace to a secular event conducted in an atmosphere of frivolity, and when possible, it is preferable to refrain from participating in such an event. And if there is no possibility of refraining from it, at the very least one should take care not to drink alcoholic beverages there.</p>","notes_en":[{"n":16,"en":"Mishna Sota 48a: \"When the Sanhedrin ceased – song ceased from the banquet halls, as it is stated in the prophecy of calamity in Yeshayahu (24:9): 'They shall not drink wine with a song,' etc.\" And in Gittin 7a Mar Ukva ruled that even \"zimra,\" that is, singing with the mouth, is forbidden while drinking wine, as it is stated (Hoshea 9:1): \"Rejoice not, O Israel, unto exultation like the peoples.\" In the Bavli, Sota there, they explained that the prohibition is from the time the Sanhedrin ceased, as it is stated (Eikha 5:14): \"The elders have ceased from the gate – the young men from their music.\" And they explained in the Yerushalmi, Sota 9:12: \"At first, the awe of the Sanhedrin was upon them, and they would not say obscene words in song. But now that the awe of the Sanhedrin is not upon them, they say obscene words in song.\" And the Rambam (Hilkhot Ta'aniyot 5:14) and SA OḤ 560:3 wrote without qualification that the prohibition is because of mourning for the Destruction.<br>But songs and praises to God may be sung while drinking wine, and likewise at a joyous occasion of a mitzva such as a wedding. That is, even before the Destruction it was forbidden to sing coarse and frivolous words over wine, but it was permitted to sing ordinary secular songs over wine, and after the Destruction even \"songs of a person's love for his fellow and of praising the beautiful for his beauty\" were forbidden over wine, as Rav Hai Gaon wrote (Teshuvot Ha-geonim, Harkavy 60), and his words were quoted by the Rif (Berakhot, beginning of chapter 5) and Tosafot Ha-Rosh (Gittin 7a)."}]}]});
